          "id": "0de30277c4ef1820d0801a67fafd186c329a63c1",
          "message": "fix(mysql): store identifiers with a case-sensitive (binary) collation (#3161)\n\n## Summary\n\nSpiceDB identifiers are case-sensitive, matching the Postgres,\nCockroachDB, and Spanner datastores. On MySQL, however, every table was\ncreated with the utf8mb4 default collation (`utf8mb4_0900_ai_ci`), and\n`relation_tuple` was later moved to latin1 (`latin1_swedish_ci`) — both\n**case-insensitive**. These incorrectly collide identifiers that differ\nonly in letter case (e.g. `Foo` and `foo`) in the unique indexes and in\nlookups. That is both a correctness bug (relationships for distinct\nobject IDs collide / leak across cases) and a performance cost\n(case-insensitive collations compare via weight tables, while binary\ncollations are a plain byte compare).\n\nThis converts every stored string column to a binary, case-sensitive\ncollation.\n\n## ⚠️ Migration warning\n\nMigration `0013` runs `ALTER TABLE ... CONVERT TO` / `MODIFY` against\n`relation_tuple` and the schema tables. On large MySQL instances this\n**rebuilds the `relation_tuple` table in place**, which can take a long\ntime and hold a metadata/table lock for the duration. Run the upgrade in\na low-traffic window, or apply it with an online schema-change tool\n(e.g. gh-ost) on big datasets.\n\n## What changes\n\n| Table | Column(s) | New collation |\n|---|---|---|\n| `relation_tuple` | all string cols (object/subject IDs, types,\nrelations, `caveat_name`) | `latin1_bin` |\n| `relationship_counter` | `name` | `utf8mb4_bin` |\n| `namespace_config` | `namespace` | `utf8mb4_bin` |\n| `caveat` | `name` | `utf8mb4_bin` |\n| `stored_schema` / `stored_schema_revision` | `name` | `utf8mb4_bin` |\n| metadata | `unique_id` | `utf8mb4_bin` |\n\n- `relation_tuple` stays on **latin1** because its `VARCHAR(1024)`\nobject-id columns would exceed InnoDB's 3072-byte index key limit at\nutf8mb4 (4 bytes/char); every other column fits utf8mb4 and uses\n`utf8mb4_bin`.\n- The two **reachable** mixed-case identifiers are object/subject IDs\nand relationship counter names (counter names have no lowercase API\nconstraint). Object types, relation names, and caveat names are\nlowercase-restricted by protovalidate, so converting those columns is\ndefense-in-depth / consistency.\n- The migration-version tracking table is intentionally left untouched\n(framework metadata, not SpiceDB data).\n- SpiceDB never JOINs string columns across tables, so the mixed\n`latin1_bin`/`utf8mb4_bin` collations cannot produce \"illegal mix of\ncollations\" errors. Converting case-insensitive → case-sensitive only\nrelaxes uniqueness, so it cannot violate an existing unique constraint.\n\n## Tests\n\nAdds cross-datastore regression tests (run against every datastore):\n- `CaseSensitivityTest` — object IDs differing only by case are distinct\nrelationships.\n- `CounterCaseSensitivityTest` — counter names differing only by case\nare distinct counters.\n\nFull `mage testds:mysql` suite passes.",
